# Symptom-specific guidance

Read only the affected topic. These are discovery aids, not universal installation
requirements, prescribed frameworks or authority to change unrelated application code.
Real ownership, finite execution and applicable regression/approval rules still apply.

- **Blank preview:** trace actual bind address/port and preview/proxy route. A frontend
  fallback document with HTTP 200 is not proof that the API is healthy. Verify the
  actual routed health contract and startup state before changing ports.
- **Browser/e2e startup:** establish the harness's real startup order. Approved cleanup
  must precede creating new servers, not kill them afterward. Playwright's webServer
  normally runs before globalSetup; verify the installed configuration. Pass browser
  initialization values explicitly where closure serialization loses them.
- **Generated-file races:** map regenerators of the same outputs to the same lease.
  Preserve failure evidence. Do not attribute partial outputs to source bugs or rerun
  until green without the governing diagnostic permission.
- **Database pool crashes:** inspect real error-event and shutdown behavior when pools
  are involved. Correct handlers/lifecycles only within scoped repair; do not add a DB.
- **Fake-timer leakage:** inspect framework isolation and clock restoration when the
  symptom involves TTL/cache/time. Preserve existing cross-file semantics; lifecycle
  placement is not a universal Port Authority requirement.
- **WebSocket/HMR disconnects:** confirm idle/proxy behavior before adding keepalive.
  Use supported protocol pings where indicated; browser JavaScript has no native ping
  API, and JSON heartbeats are not protocol frames. Use observed appropriate limits,
  not historical universal 20/30-second defaults. Clean up timers/connections and verify
  reconnection through the actual route.
- **Workflow-capacity rejection:** compare actual inventory and limit evidence.
  Inconsistency after one permitted bounded retry is a host blocker, not permission to
  delete unrelated workflows or launch unmanaged background services.

Use actual finite request/execution bounds and safe isolated negative fixtures.
Do not disrupt production or invent fields/endpoints to make a health check appear
green. Fixture rejection proves that rejection, not real application readiness.
